  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday (Friday): Temperatures are running 5-10 degrees cooler than \nyesterday at this time with an upper level trough over the region \nadvecting in cooler air. Mid and high level clouds are beginning to \nmove in ahead of an incoming system. Cloud coverage will continue to \nincrease into the evening. \n\nSaturday and Sunday: A deeper and colder system arrives Saturday \ninto Sunday, bringing our temperatures down even further with \nafternoon highs over the weekend forecast to peak in the low to mid \n70s (upper 70s for the warmer Lewiston-Clarkston valley). In \naddition to notably cooler temperatures, this system will bring \nchances for showers and thunderstorms to the Cascades, over the \nhigher terrain along the Canadian border, and across Southeast WA \nand the central ID Panhandle. \n\nThe first chances for showers will move in late Friday night over \nthe Central ID Panhandle as a band of instability tracks out ahead \nof the low pressure system. Any precipitation with this initial band \nof instability will be light (less than 0.05 inches). An isolated \nlightning strike or two with these late night showers cannot be \nruled out, but thunderstorm chances are looking low (around 5 \npercent).\n\nChances for showers and thunderstorms increase for mountains and \nareas of higher terrain across the region heading into the daytime \nhours on Saturday as the robust low pressure system tracks through. \nThe northern mountains and highest elevations of the Cascades have a \n20+ percent chance of seeing more than 0.10 inches of rain through \nthe day Saturday. There is a 15 to 25 percent chance of accompanying \nthunderstorms in these same areas. Inverted-V model soundings \nindicate potential for gusty outflow winds with any showers or \nstorms that develop.\n\nWinds will also just be breezy in general as Saturday's system moves \nthrough, even where there are no showers or storms. Expect \nwest/southwest winds with gusts of 20-30 mph across the Basin, \nPalouse, Spokane/CdA, Waterville Plateau, and Wenatchee areas. \nSunday will be less windy and largely dry with chances for \nprecipitation confined to the northern ID Panhandle as the weather \nsystem shifts east out of the region. \n\nIt's also worth mentioning that low temperatures are forecast to \nfall into the mid 30s to 40s Saturday night into Sunday morning, \npotentially leading to frost development for some of the colder \nsheltered valleys. Those in sheltered valleys may also wake up to \nfog Sunday morning thanks to light winds and residual low level \nmoisture from Saturday's showers.\n\nMonday to Wednesday: A progressive weather pattern continues into \nnext week. A brief return to warm and dry conditions is expected \nMonday and Tuesday under a transient ridge of high pressure, then \nanother system moves in by Wednesday, bringing back cooler \ntemperatures and additional chances for precipitation. /Fewkes",
